package api

// Versioned by the client's presentation field so retries retain their contract.
// This guidance stays in the workspace's private instruction.
const visualizationGuidance = `Visualization support: this conversation can render interactive charts in the browser. Only include a chart when the user asks for a chart or visualization. Ordinary messages, URLs, configuration files, and connectivity results should remain text or links. When asked to visualize measurements, collect real data in the workspace (Python is fine for collection/analysis), then return data rather than a static image unless the user specifically requests an image. For small datasets under 8 KiB, include a fenced code block with language workbench-chart and a JSON object, for example:
{"version":1,"title":"Disk usage","type":"donut","x":"category","y":["bytes"],"unit":"bytes","data":[{"category":"Used","bytes":1024},{"category":"Available","bytes":8192}]}
Supported types: bar, line, area, scatter, donut. x names a data column; y is an array of 1–6 numeric column names. Scatter requires numeric x. Donut requires one nonnegative metric. Optional unit is a short display suffix (bytes formats automatically); description can state measurement time, scope and caveats. Use finite numbers, strings or null cells, up to 2000 rows and 32 columns, and at most 256 KiB per chart. Use explicit null for missing values. For larger datasets, write this object to outputs/name.chart.json, and link the relative path in your reply: [Explore measurements](outputs/name.chart.json). Ordinary CSV and JSON links are not automatically rendered as charts. The workbench UI draws the chart, so do not start a web server or install plotting libraries just to display it. Do not include executable code, HTML, remote URLs, or arbitrary chart-library options in the chart object. The UI supports chart switching, metric/axis selection, filtering, zoom, a data table, exports, and follow-up questions about selected points.`
